# refresh-admin-cred.ps1
$user    = "DOMAIN\adm_gagan"                     # your admin account
$servers = Get-Content "$PSScriptRoot\servers.txt" # one hostname per line

$sec   = Read-Host "Admin password" -AsSecureString
$plain = [Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::PtrToStringAuto(
           [Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::SecureStringToBSTR($sec))

foreach ($s in $servers) {
    cmdkey /generic:"TERMSRV/$s" /user:$user /pass:$plain | Out-Null
    Write-Host "updated $s"
}
$plain = $null




1. Let ASG use what cmdkey already stores (test this first). ASG is built on Microsoft's RDP control, which can pick up TERMSRV/<server> entries from Windows Credential Manager when the connection itself has no credentials set. To test:

Clear the saved credentials on one ASG connection.
Make sure cmdkey /list shows TERMSRV/thatserver.
Connect.

If it logs straight in, you only ever update cmdkey, and the script below handles that daily.

2. Use one shared credential object in ASG. ASG lets you create a credential object and have all connections reference it, through folder inheritance or by assigning it. Your daily update then becomes a single 10-second edit in one place, not per connection. That's the low-tech fix if option 1 doesn't work.

3. UI automation. A pywinauto script could open that credential object and paste the new password. It works, but it breaks whenever ASG updates its UI. I'd only build it if options 1 and 2 both fail.

Daily cmdkey refresh. This prompts once, never writes the password to disk, and updates every server:

You can trigger it from a desktop shortcut or a Task Scheduler task at logon. It has to be interactive, because a scheduled task can't know the new password.

If the password changes daily because a PAM tool like CyberArk rotates it, that rotation is a deliberate control. Caching it locally in cmdkey or ASG may conflict with the policy behind it. If your shop has CyberArk PSM or a PAM connector for RDP, that's the sanctioned way to get the same convenience.
  
